.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_mp \- declare and create an ordered map that takes its memory from a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_mp(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map that takes all of its memory from
mmgmt_procs.

The macro is cbmap_construct(3) plus an allocator, and it uses the default
order for key_t. cbmap_construct_cc(3) replaces the order instead, and
cbmap_construct_full(3) takes both. cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) is the same
macro with a cleanup attribute, which destroys the map at the exit of the
scope.

cbmap_declare(3) followed by cbmap_init_mp(3) is the two-step form. Use it
when the caller must handle a creation failure and the failure must not be
fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key, and
             tells the container which order to give the tree
val_t        Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the
             library never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must not be NULL
The map copies the procedures into itself, so the ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of
the caller does not have to outlive the call; the procedures themselves,
however, must stay callable for the whole life of the map
Every tree node that the map allocates later comes from these procedures,
and everything that the map frees goes back to them
The allocator has no effect on the order: key_t decides the order, as
cbmap_create_full(3) describes, and the walk goes in ascending key order
Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3). The companion type variables follow
the scope of the handle, so a function that receives the handle needs
cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

cbmap_construct_mp(sorted, int, char *, &procs);
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
cbmap_destroy(sorted);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_full (3),
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
